---
# ytt Secret Template — for OpenBao, NOT for direct kubectl apply
#
# ytt federates to the org's self-hosted Authentik (sso.ardenone.com) with
# its own OAuth2 client (ADR-003, jedarden/ytt docs/plan/plan.md) — it does
# NOT share a credential with ibkr-mcp the way the earlier Google-federated
# setup did.
#
# OpenBao path:
#   secret/ardenone-cluster/ytt/oauth
#
# Populate via CLI:
#   bao kv put secret/ardenone-cluster/ytt/oauth \
#     client_id="<Authentik-issued or self-generated client id>" \
#     client_secret="<Authentik-issued or self-generated client secret>"
#
# This is read by TWO consumers, which is what keeps them from drifting:
#   - ytt-externalsecret.yml (this dir) -- what ytt itself PRESENTS
#   - ../authentik/authentik-oidc-clients-externalsecret.yml -- what
#     Authentik's blueprint-declared provider EXPECTS (via the !Env tag in
#     authentik-blueprints-configmap.yml's ytt.yaml entry)
#
# Values can be either:
#   (a) generated by Authentik automatically on first blueprint apply, then
#       copied out of the admin UI (Applications -> Providers -> ytt) once,
#       or
#   (b) generated ourselves (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 20` for client_id,
#       `openssl rand -hex 32` for client_secret) and written here first —
#       Authentik's blueprint reconcile then adopts these values instead of
#       generating its own, since client_id/client_secret are explicit attrs
#       (see the blueprint's !Env usage). This is the preferred path: no
#       manual UI trip, and rotation is just a `bao kv put` + resync.
#
# client_id: <see above>
# client_secret: <see above>
#
# Redirect URI registered on the Authentik provider:
#   https://mcp.ardenone.com/ytt/auth/callback
#
# ytt's own allowlist (YTT_ALLOWED_SUBJECTS, which verified emails may call
# the tools) is a plain env var in deployment.yml, not a secret — it's not
# sensitive, just an email address.
